WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:10.290 --> 00:00:19.610 align:start position:0%
 
Oh  my name is Jody Zetas and Nick Thompson  Alan and I are the two associate organ  curators at Yale and here at Yale we  look after a total of 15 pipe organs  some of them by some of the best  builders in the country if not the world  but the crown jewel of these instruments  is the one behind me the great Newberry  memorial organ here in Woolsey Hall all  of the sounds in this organ are produced  by air pressure there are no  electronically enhanced sounds or  digital sounds of any kind and it just  reflects on how clever organ builders  have been to learn new and strange ways  to shove air through organ pipes this  one has over 12,000 pipes no two of  those pipes are alike these wonderful  machines are the 19:15 Spencer turbines  that supply the organ with air pressure  and they generate enough pressure to  blow all 12,000 pipes when necessary the  air pressure is delivered to the series  of wind trunks I come out and go over  heads while bellows  and that bellows is actually a regulator  which controls the air pressure that the  organ works upon organ builders choose  the material on the shape of the pipe  depending on the kind of sound they want  to generate for example these narrow  skinny pipes are called string pipes  they just sit in the hole and they  produce a sound which is a rather sounds  like a string whereas the equivalent  pipes these flutes for example have an  entirely different sound  behind these doors is a treasure trove  of technology I'm very proud about this  is something you will not see anywhere  else in the world anymore this is the  1928 remote combination action for the  organ it was very sophisticated  technology of its time it's the space  shot of its period and basically the  combinations that the organist chooses  to recall what the push buttons are  stored down here on what amounts to an  electro pneumatic computer this is a  series of unions for the wiring of the  organ and all the names of the stops  were carefully written here in ink back  in 1915 I like looking at it because  everything here is original and you see  the care that was exercised in the  wiring of the organ  these wires are double cotton covered  wires and they were shellac for  protection  they'll be here a hundred years from now  in this mysterious door is the back of  the organ console and when necessary I  can climb out there and make repairs to  the organ console without having to pull  it away from the stage some of the pipes  are as tall as a four-story house others  are the size of a swizzle stick some of  them are extremely soft in nature and  delicate and make you think that there  are angels floating in the ceiling other  sounds are so powerful that they are  overwhelming and cascades of sound  engulf the listeners in this hall it's a  sound that I've never grown tired of

